  "notes": {
    "heritage": "The EK31 engine represents Subaru's entry into automobile manufacturing. Drawing from Fuji Heavy Industries' aircraft heritage (formerly Nakajima Aircraft), the engine was designed to be lightweight and efficient. The Subaru 360 became known as the 'Ladybug' due to its rounded shape and helped mobilize post-war Japan.",
    "design_philosophy": "Maximum efficiency from minimum displacement - the 360 was designed to meet Japan's Kei car regulations (360cc maximum displacement) while providing practical transportation for families.",
    "reputation": "Despite its small size, the EK31 proved remarkably durable. Many Subaru 360s accumulated over 100,000 km with basic maintenance. The two-stroke design meant simple servicing but required premix fuel.",
    "common_issues": [
      {
        "issue": "Crankshaft bearing wear",
        "severity": "Medium",
        "cause": "Normal wear, especially with improper oil mixture",
        "prevention": "Use correct oil premix ratio, quality two-stroke oil"
      },
      {
        "issue": "Carburetor jetting",
        "severity": "Low",
        "cause": "Altitude and temperature changes affect mixture",
        "prevention": "Re-jet carburetor for local conditions"
      },
      {
        "issue": "Cooling fan belt wear",
        "severity": "Medium",
        "cause": "Normal wear, critical for air-cooled engine",
        "prevention": "Regular inspection, replace every 20,000 km"
      },
      {
        "issue": "Exhaust port carbon buildup",
        "severity": "Medium",
        "cause": "Two-stroke combustion byproduct",
        "prevention": "Periodic decarbonization, use quality oil"
      }
    ],
    "maintenance_notes": "Two-stroke maintenance differs from modern engines. Always use fresh premix fuel, never store premixed fuel for more than 30 days. Spark plug condition indicates mixture accuracy - light tan coloring is ideal."
  },
